        "pkcontributionId": 65418,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 399,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> compatible architectural patterns, cellular detail, and immunohistochemistry.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:33:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65419,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 399,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The staging is similar to that of vulvar cancer.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:33:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 65420,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 399,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Treatment is local excision with adjuvant chemotherapy. Vulvar recurrence is seen mostly within 1&nbsp;year of diagnosis, frequently accompanied by metastasis to lymph node (inguinal, pelvic, or mediastinal), bone, or lung/pleura {26076163; 27959583}. The recurrent or metastatic lesions are controlled by excision and chemotherapy {26076163}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-09T16:33:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]